    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-213180",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Let $A={1,2,3}.$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y=4}?$",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $x+y=4.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,3),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,1),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14120255/assets/3dec67ac3a21ca9db5967f5e650fd79d6aaadab05c900db24b7a9669a872f2f2.png)",
